{"answer":"Let S_n=1+3+\\cdots+(2n-1). For n=1, S_1=1=1^2. If S_n=n^2, then S_{n+1}=S_n+(2(n+1)-1)=n^2+2n+1=(n+1)^2. Hence, by induction, S_n=n^2 for every positive integer n.","approaches":[{"description":"Mathematical induction using the next odd summand.","id":"induction-proof","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The base case and inductive step establish the stated identity for all positive integers."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in base-case step and inductive step.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"odd-sum-identity","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every positive integer n, 1+3+5+...+(2n-1)=n^2.","step_ids":["base-case","inductive-step","induction-conclusion"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"base-case","justification":"For n=1, the sum is 1, which equals 1^2.","statement":"The identity holds for n=1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"inductive-step","justification":"Assuming 1+3+...+(2n-1)=n^2, adding the next summand gives n^2+(2n+1)=(n+1)^2.","statement":"If the identity holds for a positive integer n, it holds for n+1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["base-case","inductive-step"],"id":"induction-conclusion","justification":"The principle of mathematical induction applies to the base case and the implication from n to n+1.","statement":"The identity holds for every positive integer n."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
